    <p class="title is-5 mathjax">
      
        FD-DiT: Frequency Domain-Directed Diffusion Transformer for Low-Dose CT Reconstruction
      
    </p>
    <p class="authors">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Authors:</span>
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Liu%2C+Q">Qiqing Liu</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wei%2C+G">Guoquan Wei</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Zhou%2C+Z">Zekun Zhou</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Wen%2C+Y">Yiyang Wen</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Shi%2C+L">Liu Shi</a>, 
      
      <a href="/search/?searchtype=author&amp;query=Liu%2C+Q">Qiegen Liu</a>
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="abstract mathjax">
      <span class="search-hit">Abstract</span>:
      <span class="abstract-short has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2506.23466v1-abstract-short" style="display: inline;">
        &hellip;exposure but suffers from image artifacts and loss of detail due to quantum and electronic noise, potentially impacting diagnostic accuracy. Transformer combined with diffusion <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> has been a promising approach for image <span class="search-hit mathjax">generation</span>. Nevertheless, existing methods exhibit limitations in preserving finegrained image d&hellip;
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2506.23466v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'inline'; document.getElementById('2506.23466v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'none';">&#9661; More</a>
      </span>
      <span class="abstract-full has-text-grey-dark mathjax" id="2506.23466v1-abstract-full" style="display: none;">
        Low-dose computed tomography (LDCT) reduces radiation exposure but suffers from image artifacts and loss of detail due to quantum and electronic noise, potentially impacting diagnostic accuracy. Transformer combined with diffusion <span class="search-hit mathjax">models</span> has been a promising approach for image <span class="search-hit mathjax">generation</span>. Nevertheless, existing methods exhibit limitations in preserving finegrained image details. To address this issue, frequency domain-directed diffusion transformer (FD-DiT) is proposed for LDCT reconstruction. FD-DiT centers on a diffusion strategy that progressively introduces noise until the distribution statistically aligns with that of LDCT data, followed by denoising processing. Furthermore, we employ a frequency decoupling technique to concentrate noise primarily in high-frequency domain, thereby facilitating effective capture of essential anatomical structures and fine details. A hybrid denoising network is then utilized to optimize the overall data reconstruction process. To enhance the capability in recognizing high-frequency noise, we incorporate sliding sparse local attention to leverage the sparsity and locality of shallow-layer information, propagating them via skip connections for improving feature representation. Finally, we propose a learnable dynamic fusion strategy for optimal component integration. Experimental results demonstrate that at identical dose levels, LDCT images reconstructed by FD-DiT exhibit superior noise and artifact suppression compared to state-of-the-art methods.
        <a class="is-size-7" style="white-space: nowrap;" onclick="document.getElementById('2506.23466v1-abstract-full').style.display = 'none'; document.getElementById('2506.23466v1-abstract-short').style.display = 'inline';">&#9651; Less</a>
      </span>
    </p>
    

    <p class="is-size-7"><span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Submitted</span> 29 June, 2025; 
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">originally announced</span> June 2025.
      
    </p>
    
    <p class="comments is-size-7">
      <span class="has-text-black-bis has-text-weight-semibold">Comments:</span>
